## Documentation Standards (this repo)

### RELEASE_NOTES.md format

Version header: `## vX.Y -- Title (YYYY-MM-DD)`. Standard sections (include only applicable): Bug fixes, New features, Improvements, Documentation, Files created. Each as a table. End with `**Verified on:**` line.

### Version numbering

`major.minor.patch` (e.g., v0.14.1) -- major = structural, minor = features/tools, patch = isolated bug fixes. Multiple changes same day roll into one release.

### ROADMAP.md format

Three sections: In Progress, Planned, Completed. Table: `| Item | Plan | Priority | Summary |`. In Progress must link `plans/*.md`. Completed items move to RELEASE_NOTES.md.

### Reference doc threshold

Content >20 lines of detail goes in `reference/`, not CLAUDE.md. Keep CLAUDE.md under 200 lines.

### When to create a plan

Spans multiple sessions, has phased gates, or needs detailed design beyond a roadmap row.

### Layered reference architecture

- **Rules** (`.claude/rules/` / `.cursor/rules/`) = concise behavioral directives, ~40-100 lines
- **References** (`reference/`) = implementation detail, unlimited length
- **No duplication** — one authoritative location per fact
- **Tool configs** documented per-tool in `@reference/tool-registry.md`
- **Specs vs state** — rules and references are specs; `@reference/incidents.json` tracks out-of-spec code
- **Cursor parity** — `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` mirrors `.claude/rules/*.md`. See `@.claude/rules/cursor-rule-parity.md`

### `@` link convention

- `@` can reference ANY repo file: `@reference/`, `@.claude/rules/`, `@shared/`, `@scripts/`, `@plans/`
- Use `@` consistently for all cross-references to signal "this is a linked file" and enable grep-based link auditing

### Function and library attribution

- Reference shared library functions by file + function name: `deploy_managed_file()` in `@scripts/aitools-lib.sh` / `Deploy-ManagedFile` in `@scripts/aitools-lib.ps1`
- Never cite line numbers — they drift. Function names are stable identifiers
